# Project Generation Flow

## Purpose

This doc covers the current create/canvas generation flow: canvas session persistence, prompt assist, image generation, video generation, sound generation, and archive handoff.
Read this before changing `CreateScene`, `node-canvas-v2`, `/api/canvas`, or provider-backed generation routes.

## Main entry files

- `apps/web/app/create/page.tsx`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/CreateScene.tsx`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/create/useCreateSceneController.ts`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/create/node-canvas-v2/index.tsx`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/create/node-canvas-v2/hooks/useCanvasPersistence.ts`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/create/node-canvas-v2/hooks/useVideoPolling.ts`
- `apps/web/components/scenes/create/node-canvas-v2/lib/uploadCanvasImage.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/canvas/[projectId]/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/canvas/[projectId]/images/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/canvas/[projectId]/nodes/[nodeId]/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/canvas/upload-url/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/image/generate/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/prompt/assist/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/video/generate/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/video/status/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/video/finalize/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/api/bgm/generate/route.ts`
- `apps/web/app/lib/bgm/generationRecord.ts`
- `apps/web/lib/recoverBgmGeneration.ts`

## Main data boundaries

- The main client authority is `useCreateSceneController.ts`.
- This controller owns:
  - composing state
  - asset upload state
  - element preparation state
  - autosave timers
  - generation task state
  - publish handoff state
- Local persistence boundaries:
  - `NODE_CANVAS_STORAGE_PREFIX` in localStorage
  - `REFERENCE_TAGS_STORAGE_PREFIX` in localStorage
  - several sessionStorage flags such as `archive_toast` and `upload_complete`
- DB and storage persistence boundaries:
  - `projects`
  - `project_reference_images`
  - `project_asset_elements`
- `generated_videos`
- `canvas_generated_audio` (sound generations; `kind` splits music / SFX)
- Supabase storage bucket `project-assets`
- Supabase storage bucket `generated-videos`
- `/api/image/generate` now selects the backing image provider by model (including gated `gpt-image-1.5` support and older Nano Banana models).
- Selectable OpenAI image models are `gpt-image-2.5-flare` (default), `gpt-image-2.5-sunburst`, and `gpt-image-2`. The two 2.5 variants bill identically — same per-token price, same output-token rungs — so the only difference a user sees is speed: Sunburst runs 1.78× slower on generation (39.8s vs 22.4s) and 1.9× slower on edits (43.0s vs 22.7s), which is why Flare is the default. The ticket's original "generate with Flare, edit with Sunburst" split was **not** implemented: `/v1/images/edits` is selected by whether input images are present, not by model, so a per-path split needs its own adapter branch — and Sunburst's claimed edit-accuracy advantage is still an unverified vendor claim. **`gpt-image-1.5` was taken off the catalog on 2026-09-16** — measured against 2.5 it is not a cheaper option (its own rungs, 1,056 / 4,160 output tokens at $32/1M, price to 60 / 220 credits and land *between* 2.5's five rungs of 10 / 20 / 90 / 160 / 350), it bills 6.7% more per token, and it offers only three aspect ratios — no 16:9 or 9:16. The adapter and the credit table still carry it so nodes that persisted the model id keep pricing correctly in admin history; `ImageNode` falls such nodes back to the default model.
- OpenAI image models should stay hidden until API Organization Verification is complete and both `OPENAI_GPT_IMAGE_ENABLED=true` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_OPENAI_GPT_IMAGE_ENABLED=true` are set alongside `OPENAI_API_KEY`.
- Legacy create persistence (`projects.node_canvas_document`, `projects.reference_tag_ids`, `project_shots`, `project_reference_elements`) was removed on 2026-04-13.
- The remaining create runtime still contains client-side legacy node-canvas code, but the removed DB-backed endpoints now return `410 Gone` until flow-v2 is rebuilt.

## Main route boundaries

- Canvas session
  - `GET/PUT /api/canvas/[projectId]`
  - `POST /api/canvas/upload-url`
  - `POST /api/canvas/[projectId]/images`
  - `DELETE /api/canvas/[projectId]/nodes/[nodeId]`
- Generation
  - `POST /api/prompt/assist`
  - `POST /api/image/generate`
  - `GET /api/image/generate?idempotencyKey=…` (result recovery when the response is cut)
  - `POST /api/video/generate`
  - `GET /api/video/status`
  - `POST /api/video/finalize`
  - `POST /api/bgm/generate`
  - `GET /api/bgm/generate?idempotencyKey=…` (result recovery when the response is cut)
- Downstream archive / publish state
  - `GET/PATCH /api/projects/[projectId]/detail` (canvas publish-state join endpoint; no project-detail scene)

## Video edit node (modal overlay / trim)

- Text overlays in **preview** use the same raster metrics as **compose** (`overlayTextRaster`): the modal generates data-URL previews while `index.tsx` uploads PNGs before `klipse-cut` compose.
- `VideoEditModal` keeps width/height normalized to the raster bbox; inner auto-dimension sync updates do not add undo steps (`record: false`).
- Overlay boxes can be focused in the preview; **arrow keys** nudge position (Shift = larger steps) when focus is not in an input/editor.
- Result-track clips can now independently keep or mute source audio before compose; the muted state is persisted in the canvas edit node and must be honored by the compose backend.
- `VideoEditModal` v2 uses `videoTracks[].clips` as the primary edit model: the top result bar is a read-only computed summary, preview playback follows computed result segments, same-track clips are kept non-overlapping, and deleted timeline clips move to a restorable trash sidebar.
- Selecting a timeline clip enables the toolbar crop action. The crop modal writes center-crop metadata to the selected `TimelineClip` using existing `cropAspect` and `zoom`; crop size is represented by `zoom`, so preview and `klipse-cut` compose must continue honoring those fields without adding a separate persisted size field.
- Clip fade-in/out is emitted only on computed result segments that touch the original clip edge. Adjacent result segments must not merge when their covered lower-priority clips differ, otherwise compose may send a too-short background layer and produce video/audio duration drift.
- Transitions (`TimelineTransition`) are found only by the clip ids of two consecutive result segments (`fromClipId` → `toClipId`), in preview and in compose alike. The two clips may sit on different tracks — an upper-track clip covering the middle of a lower one makes two boundaries with it. Whether two segments form such a boundary is `segmentsMeet` in `lib/videoEdit/resultSegments.ts`, shared by the ruler transition markers, the mobile transition button and the split rule below. They are part of the edit node state: `EditNode` passes them to the modal as `initialTransitions`, and both close (`onCloseEditModal`) and compose (`handleEditProcess`) write them back. The close callback has one shape, `VideoEditCloseHandler` in `types.ts`, shared by the modal's `onClose` and the node. Forward it whole (`(...closed) => …`) instead of re-listing the arguments — a seven-argument forward once dropped transitions on every canvas reopen, and a render after the reopen dropped them from the result. Timeline markers (M) are stored on the edit node too (`markers`), on close and on compose; they do not change the render, so even an unchanged close hands back the current markers.
- Cutting a clip in two follows one rule in every path: the playhead split (`splitTimelineClipAtPlayhead`), the overwrite drop and the insert drop. The cut point gets neither a clip fade nor a transition. The left piece keeps the fade-in and the right piece the fade-out; by default the incoming transition stays with the left piece and the outgoing one with the right piece. The split functions leave `ClipSplit` records and `transitionsAfterSplits` moves each transition to the piece that meets its partner in the result after the edit (`clipsMeetInTracks` — hidden tracks count as shown, since hiding a track is temporary) — with an upper-track partner, or the dragged clip itself as the partner, that can be the left piece even for an outgoing transition. Only when no piece meets does the incoming transition go to the piece holding the original start edge and the outgoing one to the piece holding the end edge. A piece with a new id whose transition is not moved loses it without any error.
- Ken Burns moves linearly over a clip, and in compose over each result segment (klipse-cut zoompan); the preview also uses segment progress. A piece that plays on its own therefore restarts the motion unless it gets its own part of the line. A split gives each piece its sub-range (`kenBurnsBetween`; an overwrite that removes the middle of a clip skips the removed part), and `resultSegmentToLegacyClip` gives every result segment of a clip its sub-range. One clip becomes several segments when an upper track covers part of it or when the lower-track clip under it changes (`coveredClipIds` differ). An overwrite that covers only one side trims like an edge handle and keeps the whole line. For a node rendered before this with such a clip, the editor's change check (it compares `kenBurns`) enables 「편집 적용」 as soon as the editor opens. The canvas badge does not change, because the render signature has no `kenBurns` — unless a changed close drops the gap clips, which raises it.
- Transition length is fitted before compose and in the preview (`fitTransitionDurations` and `transitionsAtBoundaries`, `lib/videoEdit/transitionTiming.ts`) to what klipse-cut can play. Each transition is at most 80% of either neighbouring segment and leaves at least one frame of it; when the two transitions of one segment together exceed the segment minus one frame, both shrink in proportion to fit, so the xfades neither overlap nor eat into the previous segment even if klipse-cut measures a piece one frame shorter. A transition under 0.05 s is sent as no transition, the shortest first and one at a time, and a dropped transition frees its share. klipse-cut clamps on its own as well, but its left limit is 80% of all output so far and its 0.05 s floor can be longer than a one-frame piece; run through its own chain on ffmpeg 7.1, that drops the piece, cuts frames from the clip before it and leaves the audio shorter than the video. Compose takes the boundaries from the segments it actually sends (`segmentClips`), so a clip skipped for having no source does not shift them.
- Overlay and audio-track times are moved into output time before compose (`editorToOutputTime`, `overlayToOutputTime`, `audioClipsToOutputTime`, same module). klipse-cut overlaps two segments by the transition length, so its output is shorter than the editor timeline, and it draws overlays and places audio tracks at the times it receives; its own remap runs only when probed lengths differ, assumes overlap-adjusted times, ignores clip speed and does not move audio. Each transition window [B − d, B + d] of the editor is compressed into [B − d, B] of the output and later times move up by d — continuous and monotonic, so text after a transition stays with its picture and text across a transition keeps its order. Inside a window the mapping is an approximation: text aligned to the cut appears mid-crossfade (B − d/2). Fade-in and fade-out are moved with the same mapping, so a fade inside a window shrinks with it and a fade outside keeps its length; a fade longer than its overlay never completes, so it is scaled with the overlay instead and reaches the same opacity at the overlay's edge (unchanged without transitions). An overlay is never shorter than one frame, and a fade-out never starts before 0 s: klipse-cut starts it at `end − fade_out`, and ffmpeg rejects a negative start (checked on the fade filter with ffmpeg 7.1), so an overlay that ends before its own fade-out length would fail the render. Audio clips move only their start and keep their length, because klipse-cut plays `end − start` of the source and a compressed end would cut speech off; an audio clip still stops at the next clip's start on the same track and at the output end, and a clip that would start at or after the output end is not sent (klipse-cut reads a zero length as no trim). Audio is not overlapped, so a clip that spans transitions falls behind the picture by their sum — 1.0 s after two 0.5 s transitions. Output length per confirmed clip is `confirmedClipOutputSec` (`lib/videoEdit/compositionDuration.ts`): edit timebase 30/60, divided by speed.
- Clips are placed on the timeline only from sources whose length is known (`sourceTotalFrames > 0`, which reads `durationSec` before `totalFrames`); there is no placeholder clip length. The old 150-frame placeholder is gone, and so is the effect that stretched every clip ending at frame 150 to the full source and pushed the clips after it.
- Edit compose jobs store a temporary `klipse-compose-job:<projectId|local>:<editNodeId>` localStorage key only while processing. Clear it on successful compose, and when restoring a canvas, treat any edit node with `resultVideoUrl` as completed even if stale `isProcessing` / `processingJobId` flags are present. The old `klipse-compose-job:<editNodeId>` key is still read/cleared as a migration fallback.
- Edit-node “changed” badges compare the current rendered edit signature (clips, gaps, overlays, transitions, aspect/fit mode, and manual duration) with the exact signature captured on successful compose; gap clips must remain in the synced clip list so a fresh render is not marked stale.
- Every close passes `renderClips` last: the list with gap segments that 「편집 적용」 sends to `onConfirm` (one memo serves both). The first argument has no gap segments on a changed close; on an unchanged close it is the `initialClips` the host passed in. A host that renders a closed edit without reopening the editor must render `renderClips`; rendering the first argument after a changed close collapses every gap. Rebuilding the list from `sourceRegions` is not equivalent either: that round trip drops what is not saved (a hidden track comes back visible) and changes some values (`legacyClipToTimelineClip` turns `zoom` into `cropZoom` on a cropped clip without `cropZoom`). Only the shorts stale band renders a closed edit today.
- Completed edit-node outputs expose the same right-side handoff buttons as video nodes: YouTube, Instagram, and another edit node. Completed edit outputs are materialized into one `generated_videos` row per `video_edits.id` (`provider = canvas_edit`, `provider_task_id = edit:<editId>`) so publish nodes can reuse the existing generated-video upload handoff. `POST /api/canvas/edit-session` checks that the user owns `projectId` (404 otherwise) before it writes anything, and refuses a `resultVideoUrl` that is not a storage reference with 400: a key, or an absolute URL that `isOwnStorageUrl` accepts. For an absolute URL the Supabase storage path (`/storage/v1/object/...`) is looked for only before the first `?` or `#`, so the same host with that path hidden in the query or fragment (`/functions/v1/x?/storage/v1/object/public/...`) is refused. It reads the raw text rather than `new URL().pathname`, because the parser resolves `..` and `%2e%2e` and would turn a traversal that `keyOwnership` must see as invalid into another user's clean key. The normal value is the signed URL `/api/process/status` returns for the cut result key (private CDN or S3 presigned), so the editor flow is unaffected; a local setup with no storage configured, where the result is a klipse-cut file URL, can no longer save the session.
- `klipse-cut` compose results are stored in `processed-videos` under a traceable prefix when the canvas has identity context: `compose/users/<userId>/projects/<projectId>/edits/<editNodeId>/jobs/<jobId>/<uuid>/output.mp4`. Supabase DB FK cascade does not delete Storage objects, so `/api/canvas/edit-session` explicitly removes matching `video_edits.result_storage_key` and `generated_videos.video_storage_key` values when an edit session is deleted or replaced.
- The composition length rule lives in `lib/videoEdit/compositionDuration.ts` (clips → plus overlays → manual override), not in the modal. The canvas reads it through `deriveEditOutputMeta` (`lib/editNodeOutput.ts`) so an edit node is not an opaque source: connecting `edit → video-input` runs the model's `videoInputMinSec`/`videoInputMaxSec` (and the summed-length cap) against the edit node's own output length, and the publish nodes use the same derivation for caption assist. Changing the rule in one place changes both the preview and the gating — keep them in that module.
- Edit output length is only used to **reject** a connection when it is trustworthy (`durationKnown`): clip boundaries are converted to seconds through each source's probed duration/fps, so an un-probed source leaves the length provisional and the connection is allowed with a toast instead. Output *resolution* is still left to vendor validation — the compose service, not the node data, decides the output canvas.
- When the viewport is short, the modal applies `.modalHeightConstrained`, keeps the preview floor via `VIDEO_EDIT_PREVIEW_FLOOR_PX`, and compresses the timeline height (`--video-edit-timeline-compact-height`).

## Runtime flow

### 1. Enter create flow

1. `/nodecanvas?project=` (or `?new=1`) renders `CreateScene`; bare `/nodecanvas` is the project hub. Legacy `/create` redirects to `/nodecanvas`.
2. Page-level controller and `NodeCanvasV2` initialize together.
3. If a project id exists, the canvas layer loads `GET /api/canvas/[projectId]`.
4. The response returns:
   - current canvas session document
   - existing `generated_videos`
   - latest YouTube upload summary per generated video
5. The canvas screen keeps the `/nodecanvas?project=<id>` resume contract used from archive views.

### 2. Persist canvas state

1. `useCanvasPersistence` builds a serializable canvas document from current nodes / edges / text state.
2. After debounce, it saves through `PUT /api/canvas/[projectId]`.
3. Shared/runtime-only fields are stripped before persistence.
4. Local editor state still uses short-lived local UI state, but the authoritative saved canvas session is server-backed.
5. Canvas-level direct edits support `Cmd/Ctrl+Z` and `Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+Z`/`Ctrl+Y` undo-redo for node/edge edits; text inputs and modal-specific editors keep their own scoped undo behavior.
6. Canvas viewport interaction follows a Figma-like split: plain wheel/trackpad scroll pans, pinch or `Cmd/Ctrl`+wheel zooms around the pointer, hand mode drags the canvas, and select mode keeps node selection/dragging available.
7. An empty canvas (no nodes) shows the start overlay (`CanvasEmptyState`): its heading, two large cards, 이미지 생성 and 비디오 생성, that call the toolbar's own add handlers, and on a second line one text button, 사용법 보기 (`Button` ghost). That button opens the help panel (see "Canvas help" below) on its first page. (The pulsing dot that sat on the toolbar's help button until the guide was first opened was removed on 2026-10-06: it pulsed on every visit and pointed at the same place as 사용법 보기.) It only opens: if the panel is already open it switches it to the first page instead of closing it. While the help panel is open on a wide screen, the overlay steps aside from it the same way it steps aside from the layer panel (`avoidInlineEnd`, geometry in `lib/canvasHelpPanelGeometry.ts`). It is drawn only when the host passes `onNodeGuideToggle` (`CreateScene`); landing embeds do not render the overlay at all (`showToolbar && !staticInSection`).
   - **The cards are graphics that tell an image from a video, not canvas recordings.** (Changed 2026-10-07; the earlier per-locale recordings of a node being generated read as "a canvas that already has nodes on it".) The image card is a tilted three-column grid of different stills (space); the video card is one scene with a frame strip and a progress line under it (time). Both use curated generations already published by the team: the nine stills are example images of the creative-media templates (`creative_media_templates.example_image_urls`), chosen without brand marks or third-party characters and downsized to `public/images/canvas-start/start-image-1..9.webp`; the video is a 4-second clip of four scenes from the landing model card video for Seedance 2.5 (`landing_models`) at `public/videos/canvas-start/start-video.mp4`, with a poster and six strip frames. The names sit on the media in the media foreground colour, so they do not follow the theme, and the cards are the same in every language. On hover (pointer devices only) the grid's columns drift past each other, and the video plays while the strip slides and the progress line fills; leaving reverses it and pauses the video. Without hover the cards stay at rest; with reduced motion the hover state is reached without the drift and the video does not play.
   - The name sits top-left over a progressive blur (no tint), with no description line; the accessible name is the name alone. The video card shows its first frame (the poster) at rest, plays only while a mouse pointer is over it, and returns to the first frame when the pointer leaves; it never plays under reduced motion or on touch.
   - **The overlay steps aside for the open layer panel.** The canvas owns the panel's collapsed state (`NodeLayersPanel` is controlled) and passes the panel's open edge (`lib/layersPanelGeometry.ts`, 248px, guarded against the panel's own literals by a test) to the overlay as `avoidInlineStart`. At 768px and wider the overlay shifts right only as much as the centered card row would overlap the panel (none at 1280, the full edge at 820, where the row then shrinks to the remaining width). Below 768px it does not move, because the narrow canvas layout is kept as is. Instead an empty canvas opened below 768px starts with the panel collapsed (`shouldStartLayersPanelCollapsed`, `LAYERS_PANEL_AVOID_MIN_VIEWPORT_PX` guarded against the overlay's `min-[768px]:` literal by the same test; owner decision 2026-10-06), decided once when the canvas first becomes ready and before paint. A canvas that already has nodes, one emptied later, and a panel the user opens again are left alone — reopened, the panel overlays the cards as before. The shift uses the card row width after the height cap below, and it moves with the panel's own motion (440ms on open, 240ms ease-in on close, `LAYERS_PANEL_OPEN_MOTION` / `LAYERS_PANEL_CLOSE_MOTION`, guarded against the panel's literals); under `prefers-reduced-motion` it jumps.
   - **The overlay fits between the header and the bottom toolbar.** The overlay is a size container padded by the chrome heights (`lib/canvasChromeInsets.ts`: 48 on top, 12 + 40 at the bottom, guarded by a test against the header, layer panel, toolbar and LOUIS shell values), and the card row width is also capped by that band's height, so heading through 사용법 보기 never sits under the chrome (1280x720 windows, landscape phones). The card width has a floor of 128px; once it is reached the empty state's padding and then its gaps shrink (to 0 and 8) instead of scrolling, which keeps it clear of the chrome down to a canvas about 347px tall. Below that it overlaps again.
   A first empty image or video node shows a one-time, node-attached prompt (`pickFirstNodeHintTarget`, `lib/firstNodeHint.ts`); it disappears after the first input, a result, or an added node or line, and it has a close button. The connection callout that used to appear when two nodes had no line (with every handle on the canvas pulsing) was removed on 2026-10-06: nodes made from the input and output menus arrive already connected, so its condition was rare, and when it did show it made the whole canvas blink. Dragging is still explained by the output point's tooltip.
   - **It stands next to what it talks about.** (2026-10-08; it used to float in flow coordinates 20,78 from the node's corner, so its tail pointed at the empty header area while the text said "below" and "the button on the left", and it scaled with the canvas, unreadable on a phone.) The node draws it itself through `FirstNodeHintContext`, the same way the video prompt hint works: above the prompt area with the tail pointing down at the input, or — for the Trial lone video node — just right of the Start frame button with the tail pointing left at it (`firstNodeHintAnchor`, the slot is `NodeSideButtons`' `startFrameHint`). Both are counter-scaled to stay 320px wide. When the node is less than 332px wide on screen or off screen, the bottom callout shows it instead (`videoPromptHintPlacement`), with copy that names the node and the button rather than saying "below" or "this button" (`first*CalloutBody`). Closing it is logged as `onboarding_hint_closed` and saves the `firstNodeHint` flag.
   - **The prompt only recommends what the plan allows.** Every new signup is on Trial, which cannot make a video from a prompt alone (`model_text_to_video`) or upload to YouTube (`youtube_upload`, `plan-gating-constants.ts`). `lib/onboardingHintCopy.ts` picks the body: a lone video node on a plan without `model_text_to_video` is told, in two lines, that on the current plan a video can be generated only with a start frame, and to connect a start frame image with this button (what to do once the image is ready is said by the video prompt hint that appears then); other video nodes and every image node are told to enter the output below and press the blue generate button, and that the number on the button is the credits used for this generation. The hints call it the blue generate button (파란 생성 버튼, blue generate button, 青い生成ボタン; owner's wording, 2026-10-08): the name the help panel and the FAQ use, with the colour that picks it out inside the node (a test checks the name against `help.firstVideo.steps.generate`) — until 2026-10-08 the hints alone said "the button with the number", so one button had two names. Writing the verb on the button itself was tried and dropped: the wider button pushed the video node's model chip from 「Seedance 2.0 Mini」 to 「Seedance 2....」. Each sentence is its own line. All of this copy is in the formal tone (합니다체 in Korean, です・ます in Japanese) since 2026-10-02.
   - **The prompt waits for the plan.** It is not drawn while `usePlanGating().isReady` is false (`billing.feature_flags` has not arrived, or the account has no active subscription row), because `canUse` answers true in that window and would recommend the blocked path. Waiting saves nothing: the `firstNodeHint` flag is still set only by the close button, the first input, a result, or an added node or line.
   - **The finished-video coachmark follows the same rule.** The next-step coachmark beside the output point, and its off-screen callout, mention YouTube only when the plan has `youtube_upload`; otherwise they say the video can be added to an edit. The copy names the output menu's own items in quotes (‘편집에 넣기’, ‘YouTube에 올리기’; en ‘Add to an edit’, ‘Post to YouTube’), the way the image hint quotes ‘영상으로 만들기’ — until 2026-10-06 it paraphrased them as 「편집에 추가」 and 「업로드」, which matched nothing on screen. Before the plan is known they keep the YouTube wording, since a finished video almost always comes after billing has loaded.
   - **A finished image gets the same coachmark, pointing at video.** When an image node goes from no result, or generating, to having a result (after the saved canvas has been restored) and has no outgoing line, and the `imageNextStepHint` flag is unset, the same LOUIS coachmark appears beside its right output point (where `NodeNextStepMenu` opens) and only that point pulses. It says to press the button to generate a video and that selecting "Make a video" (the menu item's own name) makes this image the first frame of the video — the start-frame path Trial can use, so it is not split by plan. Off screen, the bottom callout says the image was completed off screen, with the same "Go to node" action. Uploads count (the node is created empty and the uploaded URL arrives later); a finished node is never regenerated in place (finished image and video nodes are locked, and regenerating makes a new node), so a regenerated result counts through that new node, which starts empty. The snapshots read the raw nodes, which never carry `isGenerating` (the canvas injects it only into the nodes it renders), so the pending check in `nextStepOutputReady` sees only whether a result exists. A node brought back by paste or undo does not, because it was already finished when it entered the baseline. Completion is decided per node kind in `lib/nextStepHint.ts` (`snapshotCompletion`, `findNewlyCompletedId`), and each kind keeps its own target and flag (`hooks/useNextStepHintTarget.ts`), so seeing the image hint does not use up the video one. Opening that point's menu, drawing a line out of it, the close button, or Esc closes it and saves the flag; deleting the node only hides it. Both node kinds share the node-side wiring (`hooks/useNextStepCoachmark.ts`): side chosen when shown and again when pan or zoom stops and on resize, Esc, and a single screen-reader announcement.
   - **A video node waiting for its prompt gets one hint.** When a video node has a finished image on its start frame but no prompt, no result, no generation running and no text node feeding its prompt (`findVideoPromptHintTarget`, `lib/videoPromptHint.ts`; "running" is read from the canvas state `videoGenerations` and `imageGeneratingNodeIds`, because the raw nodes never carry `isGenerating`), and the `videoPromptHint` flag is unset, the canvas shows 「이제 영상을 생성하세요」 — 「아래에 만들고 싶은 영상을 입력하고 파란 생성 버튼을 누르세요.」. Two paths end in that state: on Trial, the first-node prompt on a lone video node sends the user to connect an image with the Start frame button, and when that image finishes nothing used to say what next (the first-node prompt is gone once there are two nodes, and the image next-step hint skips an image that already has a line out); and picking "Make a video" on a finished image creates exactly such a node. It applies to every plan. Where it appears depends on how big the node is on screen (`videoPromptHintPlacement`, owner decision 2026-10-06): when the node is visible and at least 332px wide on screen, the video node draws a coachmark just above its prompt area, in the empty preview space, with the tail pointing down at the input (box left aligned to the input so the fixed 24px tail lands on it), counter-scaled to stay 320px wide and 12px away at any zoom. Otherwise (phones, a zoomed-out canvas, or the node off screen) the bottom callout shows it with "Go to node" (`focusNodeById`) and a close button. The flag is saved when the hint is closed, or when the target node gets a prompt, starts generating or has a result; deleting the node or detaching the image only hides it.
   - **One hint at a time.** `lib/canvasHintQueue.ts` picks at most one in this order: video next step, image next step, video prompt, first-node prompt. Eligibility means "would actually be drawn now", not "has a target": a next-step target that lost its result to a regenerate is not eligible, and the next one in line takes the turn meanwhile. Nothing is drawn while a dialog is open on the canvas — the modals that switch off the delete key (`canvasModalOpen` in `index.tsx`: composition editor, analysis, crop, image edit, delete confirmation) plus the upgrade dialog, credit dialogs, confirm dialog, model-change confirmation, and the help panel. Hidden hints are not saved; the target and flag stay, so the hint returns when the dialog closes, and a hidden coachmark is unmounted so it hears no Esc. Open node menus hold the hints the same way (`nodeMenuOpen`): every `MetaDropdownPanel` on the interactive canvas reports opening and closing through `NodeMenuChromeContext`, the canvas keeps one set of open menu ids (`nextOpenNodeMenus` in `lib/nodeMenuChrome.ts`, a set rather than a count so a repeated report or a close from an unmounting node cannot drift), and while it is non-empty no hint or callout is drawn and nothing is saved; when the last menu closes the hint is picked again. The one exception keeps its old meaning: opening the next-step target's own output menu is following the hint, so `useNextStepCoachmark` calls `onMenuOpen` in the same commit, while the node is still the target, and the hint closes and is saved rather than hidden (before 2026-10-08 any other menu opened under a coachmark left the two competing for the same spot). A next-step target that finished while another hint held the turn waits the same way, with no time limit: when the turn comes it still shows on that node, because the conditions that made it the target (finished, no outgoing line, flag unset) still hold, and the off-screen callout's wording ("completed off screen") stays true however long ago it finished. If the user draws a line out of it while it waits, it closes and the flag is saved without being shown, since the user has already used the output point. Landing embeds (`showToolbar` false or `staticInSection`) draw none, including the first-node prompt, and write no flags (`onboardingCanvasMode` in `lib/canvasHintQueue.ts`; every flag write in the canvas goes through the writer `guardOnboardingFlagWrites` returns, which does nothing in an embed): they share the same `localStorage` key, and the old help guide's example canvas (removed 2026-10-07), which had lines, used to mark the (since removed) connection hint as seen just by being opened. The upgrade dialog a YouTube or Instagram node opens inside itself is not in that set; it covers the coachmark and is `aria-modal`, so its Esc still leaves the coachmark alone.
   - **The coachmark close button has no fill.** The app ships without Tailwind preflight, so a bare `<button>` keeps the UA `ButtonFace` fill and `ButtonText` color (`globals.css` resets only its border). The close button sets a transparent background and inherits the coachmark's foreground, rests at the body's 72% opacity, and goes to 100% on hover and keyboard focus. It is a 44x42 cell in the surface's inner top-right corner, so the glyph's center sits on the title line's center and nothing outside the surface presses it; it draws no focus ring (LOUIS DS-D-0074).
   - **The bottom callouts keep a 12px margin.** LOUIS caps a callout at `min(720px, var(--louis-overlay-available-width, 100vw))`; the canvas sets that variable to the canvas width minus 24px, so on a phone the callout no longer runs edge to edge with its rounded corners cut off.
   - **The off-screen callout can be closed.** Besides "Go to node" it has the same glyph close button (28px, inside the callout); it closes the hint and saves the flag like the coachmark's close button, and records `closed` with `close` under the callout's hint name.
   - **What the hints record.** Diagnostic product events go out through `hooks/useOnboardingTelemetry.ts` (decisions in `lib/onboardingHintEvents.ts`), only from the in-app canvas (`showToolbar && !staticInSection`); landing embeds send nothing. `hint` is `first_node` (`first_node_trial` for the start-frame body), `video_prompt`, `video_next_step`, `offscreen_video`, `image_next_step` or `offscreen_image`.
     - `onboarding_hint_shown { hint }` when the hint is actually drawn, once per hint per browser session (`sessionStorage`, the same unit as `product_events.session_id`), so a reload does not send it again.
     - `onboarding_hint_closed { hint, via }`: the close button on a coachmark or a bottom callout (`close`) or Esc (`esc`). Rows written before 2026-10-06 may also carry `hint: connect` and `via: got_it` from the removed connection callout. Esc is heard on `document` but only counts when nothing else used it (`escapeClosesNextStepHint` in `lib/nextStepHint.ts`): an Esc already handled (`defaultPrevented`, e.g. cancelling a layer rename), one typed inside an input, or one pressed while any `aria-modal` dialog is open leaves the coachmark alone. The upgrade and credit dialogs (`GlassConfirmModal`) stop the event in the capture phase, so it never reaches the coachmark.
     - `onboarding_hint_acted { hint, via }`: a prompt typed into the first node or into the video-prompt hint's node (`typed`); a line into the first node or a line out of the finished video or image (`connect`); the output menu opened from the coachmark (`menu`); the go-to button on the off-screen callout or the video-prompt callout (`go_to`). Closing and acting count once per hint per session, so a hint is never followed more often than it was shown. A hint that disappears for any other reason (another node added, the node deleted, the plan changed) records nothing, and a next-step hint switching between the coachmark and the off-screen callout for the same node is a move, not a disappearance (video and image are separate pairs).
     - `plan_gate_shown { feature }` each time the canvas-level upgrade dialog opens. The dialogs nodes open themselves (video model and resolution pickers, YouTube, Instagram, sound, the composition editor) do not send it.
     - The plan code goes in `plan_code` only once billing has loaded, because before that `usePlanGating().planCode` is a `trial` fallback rather than a fact; a hint drawn earlier (for example a video-prompt hint on a saved canvas that opens before billing arrives) carries none. Failures are swallowed and never block the canvas. The admin dashboard leaves all four out of its fetch and its retention (`admin-product-metrics.md`). `trackProductEvent` also sends every event to GA4 under the same name with the metadata (`hint`, `via`, `feature`) as parameters, so these four appear in the GA property too.

### 3. Prompt assist and image generation

1. Text/LLM nodes call `POST /api/prompt/assist`. The request's `targetKind` (`image` / `video`) picks the prompt-writing rules. A text→prompt connection into an image or video node decides it. Without one, the node's Image/Video toggle does (`promptPurpose`, saved with the node, empty = `image`). There is no user-facing Auto: the draft text alone gives no basis to choose, so the connection is the only automatic path. `auto` remains only for landing mockups, which have no toggle. While a connection decides the kind, the toggle is locked and says so. It is also locked once a result exists, and then shows the kind that result was made with (`generatedTargetKind`); Regenerate opens a new node with the same purpose.
   - **Output lines stack; the other kind branches.** A text node's output lines accumulate, whether the next node is made from the output menu or connected by dragging — dragging no longer deletes the node's other output lines (it used to, so an earlier node silently lost its prompt). The exception is an AI prompt whose purpose is already settled — by its result (`generatedTargetKind`), or else by the first image or video node it feeds (`settledPromptPurpose` in `lib/promptTargetKind.ts`). Connecting it to the *other* kind (image ↔ video) branches instead of drawing the line (`promptOutputAction`, `branchPromptNode`): a new AI prompt node copies the draft, model settings, and incoming edges (the same `promptNodeCloneData` / `cloneIncomingEdges` that Regenerate uses), takes the target's kind as its purpose, is placed in the first free row below the original, and writes its prompt immediately (`autoGenerateOnce`, consumed once and never saved; prompt assist costs no credits). The canvas checks the plan before planting that flag (`branchPromptNode`): without `prompt_assist` (trial) the clone is created but does not write, and the canvas-level upgrade dialog opens; while the plan is still unknown (billing not loaded) it does not write either, and the user presses Generate. The dialog lives on the canvas rather than in the node because the editor renders only visible nodes (`onlyRenderVisibleElements`): a node created off screen is rendered once to be measured and then unmounted, taking any node-local state with it. For the same reason the node writes only from an instance that is still mounted after a short settle (`AUTO_WRITE_SETTLE_MS`, 300 ms) and consumes the flag at that moment; an instance unmounted before then leaves the flag for the one `revealNewNode` brings on screen, so a two-variant answer (kept in node state) is not lost. The original node and its lines are untouched. The menu does this with a new empty node beside the clone; a drag connects the clone to the node it was dropped on. The menu's second line says which will happen (`textToImageBranchHint` / `textToVideoBranchHint`). Targets that are not image or video (TTS, another text node) always stack; sound nodes take no text input. "The first image or video node it feeds" skips lines to text or TTS nodes, since lines stack (`getConnectedPromptTargetKind`). Marketing embeds never branch.
   - New nodes from any output menu go below the lowest node already fed by that output, in the first row that keeps an 80 px gap from every existing node (`lib/nextStepPlacement.ts`); a node not yet measured counts with the measured height of its kind (`NEW_NODE_HEIGHT`) rather than the 160 px default. The text menu used to step down by a fixed 220 px, which is shorter than a video (368) or image (541) node, so new nodes landed on top of earlier ones.
2. Image nodes call `POST /api/image/generate`.
3. When the signed-in user's billing balance is known, image nodes disable Generate and show localized insufficient-credit copy before calling the API; `/api/image/generate` remains the server-side guard.
4. Generated images may be uploaded through `/api/canvas/upload-url` and then recorded via `POST /api/canvas/[projectId]/images`. The canvas calls it only when the server upload in `/api/image/generate` failed, and by then that route has closed the row as `completed` with no result. The route checks both client values before writing: `storageKey` as a relative key under `images` (`assertRelativeStorageKeyWritable`) and `imageUrl` (`assertStorageColumnWritable`), 403/503 on a key the user cannot read. With `generatedImageId` it fills only a row that has no result yet (`storage_key` null, status `generating` or `completed`); a row that already has a result or has `failed` is left as it is and its id returned, without inserting a second row.
5. Deleting a canvas node calls `DELETE /api/canvas/[projectId]/nodes/[nodeId]`, which cascade-cleans generated-image rows and storage files for that node.
6. **Unlike video, image generation holds the HTTP request open for the whole provider call.** Image providers expose no job id and no webhook, so submit-then-poll is not available and a slow generation can outlive the gateway. The ALB idle timeout cuts the connection and returns its own HTML error page while the route runs to completion — storing the image and keeping the credit capture. Measured 2026-09-11: a 62.0s `nano-banana-pro` generation came back as `ApiReturnedNonJsonError` on the client although the row was `completed`; `gpt-image-2` averages 43.6s (max 115s), so the 60s default sat inside the normal range. The ALB idle timeout is now **180s**.

   The default image model is **`gpt-image-2.5-flare`** as of 2026-09-16, and latency is most of the reason. Measured on the same output-token rungs: at 1,756 tokens Flare takes 22.4s against `gpt-image-2`'s 45.8s, and at the top rung (7,024) 57.3s against **141.9s**. The old default's slowest tier therefore ran within ~40s of the 180s ALB ceiling on a single generation; the new one halves that exposure. Editing shows the same gap (22.7s vs 52.2s). `gpt-image-2` stays in the catalog — same rungs, same cost, and no equal-tier quality comparison has been run yet.
7. When a submission gets no JSON verdict back (HTML from the gateway, a dropped connection, a throttled tab), the node does **not** fail. `recoverImageGeneration` (`lib/recoverImageGeneration.ts`) polls `GET /api/image/generate?idempotencyKey=…` every 3s for up to 240s and attaches the result exactly as the success path would; a `failed` row reports the refund the server already issued. The lookup works because the credit `reference_id` is `image-{idempotencyKey}`, a value the client owns before the request goes out.
8. That recovery lives in memory, so it dies with the page. Reloading while a generation is in flight used to lose the result **unconditionally** — the image and the capture both survived server-side, but the restored node was blank, which reads as "nothing happened" and invites a second paid attempt. Of the 8 results lost in the 30 days to 2026-09-11, 7 ended in exactly that re-charge. Two server-side paths close it, and neither needs the client to remember anything:
   - `GET /api/canvas/[projectId]` fills empty image nodes from `completed` rows (`backfillOrphanedImages`). The row already carries `node_id`. Nodes that already hold an image are never touched, so a regenerate is not rolled back, and a duplicated node gets a fresh id so a cleared result cannot come back.
   - The same route returns `generatingImages` for rows still in flight (`node_id` matched, created within 10 minutes — `maxDuration` is 300s, so anything older is a dead task, not a running one). `useResumeImageGenerations` marks those nodes generating again, keeps the elapsed clock on the row's real `created_at`, and resumes the poll above. `insertGeneratingRow` records `node_id` at capture time precisely so this is possible.
9. No cron route in `app/api/cron/` covers image (video has `cron/video-finalize`). `supabase/functions/image-refund/index.ts` is written as the image one: it takes every `generating` row older than 10 minutes, refunds it through `refund_tokens_atomic` by the row's `credit_reference_id`, and marks it `failed`. The commit that added it (`e7b06da4`) says pg_cron runs it every 5 minutes, but a pg_cron schedule is a database row and edge functions deploy on their own track (the Cron section of `docs/flows/highlight.md` records a deployed one that had drifted off the repo), so whether it runs, and which code, cannot be read from the repo. The route's comments assume it runs. If it does not, a `generating` row whose task dies keeps its capture — none observed in 60 days. If it does, it is one more refund that trusts a row's `credit_reference_id`, and RLS lets the owner insert such a row (item 10) — the image side of the open path in section 4 item 11. Its auth check refuses every call when `CRON_SECRET` is unset; it used to skip the check in that case (fail-open), so anyone who knew the function URL could run the refund sweep. That fix is in the repo only: the deployed function changes when someone redeploys it (`supabase functions deploy image-refund`), and the pg_cron job must then send `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>` or every run gets 401.
10. **A submission key is spent once.** Before charging, `POST /api/image/generate` reads the 20 most recent `canvas_generated_images` rows with the same `user_id` and `credit_reference_id`, whatever their status, and any row ends the request there: `generating` returns the existing `409 generation_in_progress`, `completed` returns the stored result in the success shape (`imageUrl`, `storageKey`, `generatedImageId`; the row keeps no `mimeType`, and the client ignores it once a URL is present), and `failed` returns `409` with `error` and the `refundedCredits` already issued. None of them captures, calls the provider, or refunds — the rule `/api/video/generate` already applies to a key that has a row. The guard used to stop only `generating` rows, which left a finished key reusable. Capture is idempotent on `reference_id`, so replaying a completed key cost nothing yet still reached the provider — a free generation — and when that call failed, the failure path refunded the same `reference_id`, handing back the **original** submission's charge: one refund per successful submission, then free generations on the same key. The decision is `decideImageSubmission` (`app/lib/generation/submissionReplay.ts`, the module video shares in section 4 item 11), pinned by its test. The canvas never takes this path: `ImageNode` holds one key per submission and clears it in `finally`, so a retry or a regenerate always sends a new key, and recovery reads with `GET`. A completed row with no URL (typically the server upload failed and the original response carried base64) comes back in the success shape with `imageUrl: null`: the image went out only in that response, so a replay has nothing to return, `ImageNode` would treat it as a failure, and nothing is regenerated or refunded. `GET` (item 7) now picks the row the same way; it used `maybeSingle`, so a key with more than one row answered `404`. The key itself must be a UUID (`isSubmissionKey`) because it goes into the `reference_id` verbatim: anything else gets `400` before the row lookup, so a key cannot be shaped into the `image-pre-{time}-{random}` reference that a request without a key gets. Such a request keeps that per-request reference as before; the canvas always sends `crypto.randomUUID()`.

    The row check is not the last gate, because the row is not a durable record. Deleting the node (`DELETE /api/canvas/[projectId]/nodes/[nodeId]`) deletes that node's rows that carry the project's `canvas_session_id`, which a row gets when its image is stored under a project (by the route or by `POST /api/canvas/[projectId]/images`); `generating` and `failed` rows, and rows made without a project, stay. The RLS policies on `canvas_generated_images` (`20260415200000`, untracked in `3c823e96`) also let the owner `DELETE` any of their rows directly and `INSERT` new ones. A replay after a deletion finds nothing. It also cannot settle two requests that carry one fresh key at the same moment — the row is written after capture, so both can find nothing. The capture ledger settles both, and the user cannot delete it. `capture_tokens_atomic` charges a `reference_id` once, and a later or losing request gets `captured: false` with the reason `already_captured`. `captureProjectUsageTokens` now passes that reason through (it used to return every uncharged result other than `insufficient_tokens` in one shape and drop the reason), and the route answers `already_captured` with the same `409` before the provider, logging a `console.warn` with the `creditRefId` — the canvas never gets here, so each one is a duplicated or crafted request. `captured: false` at a positive price with any other reason is unexplained (the RPC body is not in the repo): it gets `500` and a `console.error`, and the provider is not called either. A replay whose original never wrote its row stops there too. The decision is `decideCapture`; `unownedCaptureResponse` turns it into the response and the log. For simultaneous requests this holds only if the RPC tests `already_captured` under its lock, and the RPC body is not in the repo (`supabase/migrations/` is git-ignored, and of the two credit RPCs only the refund one is tracked). No tracked or deleted migration puts a unique constraint on `(user_id, credit_reference_id)` — the table's original migration (`20260415200000`, untracked in `3c823e96`) predates the column. Making it certain takes a migration: either the advisory lock that `refund_tokens_atomic` already takes on `(user_id, reference_id)` (`20260511110000`), added to `capture_tokens_atomic`, or that unique index with the row written before capture (existing duplicate rows removed first). Neither is written; like every migration it has to be applied by hand to prd and dev.

    **Only a request that charged refunds.** The refund RPC finds the capture by `reference_id`, so a failure path that refunds without having charged returns whatever an earlier submission under that key paid. A zero price is that case: capture skips the RPC and answers `captured: false`, which is no reason to refuse the request, but a free request that then failed refunded the earlier charge. Every refund in both routes now runs only when `chargedHere`, which is `capture.captured`, is true. No price is zero today, so this had not been reached.

### 4. Video generation

1. Video nodes call `POST /api/video/generate`.
2. The preferred path is generation-v2 compilation: request compilation → credit pre-capture → provider request → `generated_videos` insert.
   A submission the provider **rejects outright** also gets a row now: the route refunds and then writes `generation_status = "failed"` with `error_message` set to the provider signature (code + prose, the same string `mapVideoGenerationErrorToUserString` reads) and `raw_response` set to the error summary. `provider_task_id` is `NOT NULL`, so "never got one" is written as an empty string — the key-replay answer in item 11 and `/api/video/status` already treat that value as falsy. A `failed` row is outside the `cron/video-finalize` sweep (`submitted`/`processing`/`succeeded`) and makes `/api/video/status` answer from `error_message` without calling the provider. Only the request that charged writes the row (`chargedHere`), so one `credit_reference_id` keeps one row and item 11's `maybeSingle` lookup still resolves. Without this the dominant failure class was invisible: on 2026-10-01 the refund ledger held 646 `Video provider request failed` submissions across 73 users against 181 rows carrying an `error_message`, so a user could be told their input image was out of range while operations had no record of what was rejected. The one remaining hole is a row insert that itself fails (Sentry only, `Failed to create generation record` in the ledger — 21 submissions over one hour on 2026-09-30).
3. When the signed-in user's billing balance is known, video nodes disable Generate and show localized insufficient-credit copy before calling the API.
   - A prompt-only request (`resolvesToTextOnlyScenario`) on a plan without `model_text_to_video` stops in `useNodeGeneration` before the API and opens the canvas upgrade dialog through `onPlanGated(requiredPlan, feature)`. The canvas keeps the blocked feature with the dialog state (the composition-input check and the prompt-branch `prompt_assist` check fill it too) and, for `model_text_to_video` only, uses `profile.planGating.textToVideoModal` (chosen by `pickPlanGateCopy` in `lib/onboardingHintCopy.ts`): the title says a start frame is needed, and three lines say the current plan cannot generate a video from a prompt alone, to connect an image to the start frame and generate again, and which plan can. The buttons stay Close and Upgrade. Other features keep the generic copy.
   - Start frames and reference images count only once the connected image node has a result (`resolveImageNodeOutputUrlForVideo`), while the video node's generate button opens as soon as a line exists. So a line into an empty or still-generating image node resolves to a prompt-only request. When that request would be blocked and a `start-frame` or `reference-images` line exists, the canvas does not open the dialog (it would ask for what was already done); it writes `create.generateHint.awaitConnectedImage` on the video node instead (`lib/textToVideoGate.ts`). The dialog opens only when neither line exists. While the plan is unknown the client does not block, and the server's plan-limit rejection shows `create.errors.planLimitExceeded` on the node, which already named the image alternative. The dialogs that `YoutubeNode` and `InstagramNode` open themselves are separate and unchanged.
4. Credit pre-capture uses the compiled provider parameters for duration, sound, resolution, and video-input status so the insufficient-credit guard matches the actual model pricing condition.
5. Seedance 2.0 and 2.5 are available through the same generation-v2 path as provider `seedance`; they submit asynchronous BytePlus ModelArk content-generation tasks using `BYTEPLUS_ARK_API_KEY`/`ARK_API_KEY`, then poll/finalize through `/api/video/status` and `/api/video/finalize`. Seedance credit pre-capture is token-based — `(input video sec + output sec) × W × H × 24fps / 1024` at official USD-per-token rates, converted with a fixed KRW rate and margin (see `app/lib/payments/token-cost.ts`; pixel maps differ per model version). Duration is always sent explicitly because 2.5 defaults to `-1` (model-chosen 4–30s), which would break pre-capture. 2.5 first-frame requests must send `ratio: adaptive`, and 2.5 video-reference requests pin `omni_reference_task_type: reference` so prompts cannot drift into edit/extend tasks. Refunds remain tied to generation failure as with other providers.
6. Gemini Omni Flash (`gemini-omni-flash`, provider `gemini-omni`) runs on the Interactions API: `interactions.create({ background: true, store: true })` returns an interaction id that the same `/api/video/status` polling drives. Generation scenarios send `response_format.duration` (official range: integers 3–10, suffixed `s`) and `response_format.resolution` (`360p`/`720p`/`1080p`/`4k`, default 720p) — verified live 2026-09-10: `3s` → 3.008s, `7s` → 7.018s, `1080p` → 1920×1080. The edit scenario (`geminiOmni.inputVideo.edit`) sends neither `duration` nor `aspect_ratio`, so output follows the input video exactly (measured 2026-09-10: 7.018s in → 7.018s out); there `duration` is a pre-capture billing input only. **The edit input video must be sent as inline base64, never through the Files API.** Passing a `files.upload` URI lets `interactions.create` succeed and then kills the polling GET with `400 Unsupported file uri: blobstore:///…` — reproduced on both the GA and preview endpoints, with either URI form, and with `include_input: false`; the same video sent inline completes normally. A real user hit this twice in production on 2026-09-10 before it was found. Note that the omni docs *recommend* the Files API for video input, so the broken path is the documented one. The hard official limit is a 100 MB total request; our 40 MB input cap is an operating choice below it (~53 MB after base64), verified inline at 8.6 MB and 21.1 MB. Credit pre-capture is token-based per second and per resolution tier (`app/lib/payments/pricing/gemini-omni.ts`) — 1080p and 4k are documented as upscales but bill at 1.5× and 3× of 720p. A safety block arrives as an **HTTP 400 on the polling GET**, not as `status: "failed"`.
7. The response returns `generatedVideoId` plus provider task metadata.
8. `useVideoPolling` / canvas polling calls `GET /api/video/status?id=<generatedVideoId>` until the provider is terminal.
9. On `succeeded`, the client auto-calls `POST /api/video/finalize`.
10. Finalize downloads the provider asset, uploads it to storage, updates `generated_videos`, and skips token capture when the row already has a pre-capture `credit_reference_id`. It does not refund: `/api/video/finalize` only finalizes a `succeeded` row and answers `409` for any other status. The pre-capture comes back where the failure is seen — `/api/video/status` when a poll finds the task failed, and `cron/video-finalize` when the provider reports failure or the row is still unfinished two hours after it was created; the project-scoped `projects/[projectId]/generate/status` and `projects/[projectId]/generate/finalize` refund when the provider reports failure. Seedance 2.5 rows are routed through the klipse-cut `/process/transcode` queue first (its 1080p output is 10-bit HEVC that Chrome only plays with hardware acceleration); the cut worker probes the codec and merely remuxes H.264 sources, and any transcode failure falls back to uploading the original. The `cron/video-finalize` sweep applies the same gate via `app/lib/media/transcode.ts`.
11. **A submission key is spent once here too.** A key that already has a row keeps its rule: before charging, `POST /api/video/generate` reads the `generated_videos` row with the same `user_id` and `credit_reference_id` and returns its `generatedVideoId`, `providerTaskId`, and `provider` whatever its status, with no capture, provider call, or refund. Unlike image, the status does not change that answer — the response carries no result, and the client learns how the task ended by polling that id. The key must be a UUID here too, and anything else gets `400` before the lookup.

    That row is a weaker gate than image's. The success row is written only after the provider accepts the task, so the whole submission runs before it exists (a rejected submission does leave a `failed` row — item 2 — but that is a record, not a gate). The RLS policies on `generated_videos` (`20260415000000`, untracked in `3c823e96`) let the owner `INSERT` a row, `UPDATE` its `credit_reference_id`, or `DELETE` it, and deleting the project cascades it. The lookup is `maybeSingle`, so a key with two rows reads as none. A replay past the row got `already_captured` from capture — no new charge — but the route discarded that result, submitted to the provider again, and inserted a second row carrying the same `credit_reference_id`. If that task succeeded, finalize skipped capture because the row had a pre-capture reference: a free video. If it failed, the original submission's charge came back, and on video the refund does not have to happen in the route — `/api/video/status`, `cron/video-finalize`, and the two project-scoped routes in item 10 refund by the row's `credit_reference_id`, so a replayed task that the provider accepted and later failed refunded the original too. The route now answers `already_captured` with `409 generation_in_progress` before the provider, answers an unexplained uncharged result with `500`, and refunds only what it charged itself (`decideCapture` and `unownedCaptureResponse`, shared with image in `app/lib/generation/submissionReplay.ts`; section 3 item 10), so a replay never reaches the provider and never leaves a row that the status or cron path could refund. The same test pins the order inside each route's `POST`: key format, row lookup and its early return, capture, this check as the very next statement, then the provider call, with every refund behind `chargedHere`. The canvas never sends a key twice: `handleVideoGenerate` (`useNodeGeneration.ts`) makes a key per call and sends it once without retrying, so only a duplicated or crafted request sees this `409`, and the video node shows it as a generic failure. The simultaneous-request caveat in section 3 item 10 applies here unchanged.

    **Refunds outside the route still trust the row.** `/api/video/status`, `cron/video-finalize`, `projects/[projectId]/generate/status`, and `projects/[projectId]/generate/finalize` refund by whatever `credit_reference_id` the row holds, and nothing ties that reference to the row's own submission. Because RLS lets the owner insert or edit a row, a row they wrote or changed can aim that refund at any of their own `project_generation` captures — image and sound included — and once those paths decide the row's task failed, that charge comes back. A zero-price request writes its key's reference on its row too, so once a price can be zero the same path can return an earlier charge made under that key. The route changes above do not close this. It closes when the write revoke in "Generation rows are written by the server only" below is applied, because then the owner can no longer insert a row or change its `credit_reference_id`; until then it stays open.

### 5. Sound generation (BGM / SFX)

1. Sound nodes call `POST /api/bgm/generate` with `mode` (`music` / `sfx`), a duration, and the
   provider (`elevenlabs`; `mock` is the free dev path and is **not** charged, so a document whose
   node lost its `provider` would show a price the server never collects — the client defaults to
   the paid value deliberately).
2. Credits are captured atomically **before** the provider call and refunded in the failure path.
   Pricing is duration-based (`app/lib/payments/token-cost.ts`).
3. **Like image generation and unlike video, the request is held open for the whole provider call.**
   ElevenLabs Music returns the finished mp3 as the response body — there is no job id and no
   webhook — so the route downloads it and uploads it to storage inside the one request. The ALB
   idle timeout (180s) therefore cuts the connection on a long generation while **the route runs to
   completion**: the mp3 lands in storage and the credit stays captured, and only the client loses
   the result. Read item 4; the recovery machinery exists because of this.
   3a. **An SFX prompt with Korean, Japanese or Chinese text is rewritten into English before the
   provider call** (`app/lib/bgm/sfxPrompt.ts`). ElevenLabs's sound-effect model reads such a prompt
   as text to pronounce, not as a description — measured on 2026-09-17, 「찰칵」 and 「사진찍는 소리」
   came back as syllable-like speech while the same user's English shutter prompt came back as one
   click. Users read it as "I asked for a sound effect and got TTS". The rewrite uses
   `gemini-2.5-flash-lite` (deliberately not the 3.5 model prompt assist uses, so a shared-capacity
   429 on one does not take out the other), runs **after** the capture and the `generating` row so a
   refresh during it still recovers, and on any failure sends the original prompt — the behavior
   before this step. The row keeps the user's original prompt. Music prompts and the `mock` provider
   are not rewritten.
4. The client mints an `idempotencyKey` before the request and sends it with `nodeId`. The credit
   reference is `bgm-{idempotencyKey}` and a `generating` row goes into `canvas_generated_audio`
   right after capture, carrying the `node_id`. That is the whole basis of recovery — before it,
   both the storage key and the credit reference were server-side `randomUUID()` values with no row,
   so a generation that outlived the gateway left an mp3 nobody could ever find while the user paid
   for it and re-submitted. Three paths use the row:
   - `GET /api/bgm/generate?idempotencyKey=…` returns the submission's status, audio URL and (for a
     failed one) the credits already refunded. `lib/recoverBgmGeneration.ts` polls it for up to 260s
     whenever the POST did not produce a JSON verdict — the provider deadline (240s) plus the SFX
     prompt translation in item 3a (8s, twice with the backend fallback). Raise one and you must
     raise the other, or a refresh right after submitting reports a paid success as a failure.
   - A repeat POST with the same key is refused **whatever state the first one reached**: a finished
     submission answers with its stored result, and an in-flight or failed one gets **409**. Checking
     only for `generating` was a free-credit path — capture is idempotent per reference, so the
     second request is not charged, but its failure branch would refund *the first, successful*
     submission while its audio stayed in hand. The refund is therefore also gated on whether this
     request actually captured (`chargedHere`), not merely on the provider being a paid one. The key
     must be a UUID, since it becomes the ledger's `reference_id` verbatim.
   - Capture idempotency covers a *sequential* replay, not a true race: `capture_tokens_atomic` runs
     its existence check as its first statement with no lock, and `user_token_transactions` carries no
     unique constraint on `(user_id, reference_id)`, so two requests arriving in the same instant can
     both capture. Closing that needs an advisory lock in the RPC (which `refund_tokens_atomic`
     already takes) or that unique index — a migration, and it applies to every surface, not just sound.
   - `GET /api/canvas/[projectId]` returns `generatingAudio` for rows still in flight (`node_id`
     matched, created within 10 minutes) and fills empty sound nodes from `completed` rows
     (`backfillOrphanedBgmAudio`). `useResumeBgmGenerations` marks those nodes generating again and
     resumes the poll, so a refresh mid-generation no longer reads as "nothing happened".
5. The provider call carries a 240s deadline. Without one it had no upper bound at all, and since
   the refund lives in the route's `catch`, a hung call meant a capture that was never refunded and
   never produced audio. The abort message contains `timeout`, so the failure classifies as
   `generationTimedOut` and the user gets timeout copy rather than a generic failure.
6. `canvas_generated_audio` carries a `kind` column (`music` / `sfx`) rather than a table per sound
   type. TTS (`/api/tts/synthesize`) has the same loss shape and is not wired to it yet.

### 6. Archive / publish handoff

1. Archive lists project cards and routes edits back to `/nodecanvas?project=<projectId>`.
2. Canvas publish nodes use `GET/PATCH /api/projects/[projectId]/detail` to resolve or clear generated-video upload linkage.
3. Publish flows for YouTube / Instagram consume the finalized `generated_videos` rows produced by the canvas generation pipeline.
4. If the user leaves or refreshes the canvas while YouTube/Instagram uploads are still processing, node-canvas reloads publish job rows from `/api/projects/[projectId]/detail`, reattaches every matching job by `generated_videos.id` or video URL, and resumes per-node status polling instead of trusting transient client state. New uploads also persist `activeUploadJobId` on the publish node, so multiple publish nodes in the same project do not block or overwrite one another even when they point at the same generated video.

### 7. Failure reasons and credit shortage

Generation routes never show a provider's raw text. They send a **reason code** (`GenerationErrorReason`, `lib/videoGenerationErrorMessage.ts`) or a provider string that the node maps to one; the copy is picked in the browser. If the input is already a reason code, `classifyGenerationError` returns it unchanged, so a node needs no regex of its own for a code the route sends.

1. **Our provider account problem** (billing, balance, spend or usage limit, key, suspension) is `generationProviderUnavailable`. Retrying does not help and only operations can fix it, so the copy points to another model. `/api/image/generate` and `/api/video/generate` answer `500 { ok: false, error: "generationProviderUnavailable", refundedCredits }` and report to Sentry with fingerprint `["provider_unavailable", <provider>]` (`reportProviderUnavailable`). It is 500, not 503 — 503 means "retry automatically" in this codebase. Until 2026-09-30 these failures were answered as busy, and the routes returned before Sentry, so operations never saw them.
2. **Provider busy** (rate limit, overload) stays `429 { error: "provider_busy", refundedCredits }` and is shown as `create.errors.providerBusy`.
3. The verdict lives in one place. `generationFailureReason` (`app/lib/providers/failureReason.ts`) checks the account problem first, then 429 (`isRateLimitError`), then the classifier. Many account problems arrive as **429** (OpenAI `insufficient_quota`, Google prepayment depleted, Kling 1101/1102, Seedance `SetLimitExceeded`), so status alone cannot separate them from rate limits — the per-provider codes and phrases are in `PROVIDER_ACCOUNT_PATTERNS`, shared by the server and the classifier. In this verdict status 401 and 402 are account problems by themselves; 403 is not (Gemini's opaque safety refusal is a 403) and counts only with the provider's own code or phrase. fal's 403 counts only when the body says `Exhausted balance`: fal also sends `User is locked. Reason: TOP_UP.` 403s now and then with money in the account (`providers/image/fal-angle` retries any 403 three times, then throws). Sound and Google TTS are judged by their own rules and do take 403 by itself (5). Google's common sentences (suspended, billing required) count only together with the canonical status `PERMISSION_DENIED` or `RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, which `providerErrorSignature` puts back in front because unwrapping the SDK's JSON body drops it; Kling's codes count only in the top-level `code`. `failureReason.test.ts` locks one table of account, same-status busy, and near-miss examples per provider. The Kling and Seedance codes were not confirmed against the official docs (the pages render client-side).
4. **Insufficient credits** is one name everywhere: `402 { ok: false, error: "insufficientCredits", required, available }` (`app/lib/payments/insufficientCredits.ts`). The capture helpers throw `InsufficientCreditsError`; image, video, sound and highlight routes send the same body, except the highlight re-analysis `PUT` during a transition (below). The copy is `create.nodeCanvas.insufficientCreditsShort` — the video copy builder takes `create` so the same sentence is not written twice.
5. Sound (`/api/bgm/generate`) and TTS (`/api/tts/synthesize`) send reason codes only; the raw provider text goes to server logs (`providerErrorForLog`) and Sentry. Both judge by provider status first: 401, 402 and 403 are account problems, 429 and 5xx are busy. For sound, ElevenLabs's prompt rejection (`bad_prompt`, `bad_composition_plan`) wins over the status. Google TTS also reads the shared account list, since a bad key arrives as 400 (`API key not valid`). TTS spends no credits.
6. The image node maps provider failures with the video copy set but replaces the generic failure with `nodeCanvas.imageGenerationFailed`, so an unrecognised image failure no longer reads "video generation failed".
7. The booth (`/api/creative-media/generate`) uses the same verdict. It does not return the event quota on failure, except when `provider.generate` itself was rejected for an account problem: then one slot goes back by compare-and-swap (`releaseEventQuota`), which cannot lose a concurrent update. A failure while storing the result is never returned, even if it carries status 401. A failed return is logged and sent to Sentry as a warning.

A client that loaded before a deploy does not know the new codes and shows the generic failure once for an account problem. The TTS node from before this change is the exception: it prints the route's `error` as text, so until the tab reloads it shows the code itself (`generationProviderUnavailable`, `generationProviderBusy`, `generationRequestInvalid`, `generationFailed`) instead of a sentence. The highlight re-analysis `PUT` still sends `insufficient_tokens` for its 402s; the current scene reads the **status** (402), not the name (`retryChargeFailure`), so renaming it to `insufficientCredits` is safe for this code and waits only on tabs older than that change (`docs/flows/highlight.md`).
## Generation rows are written by the server only

(생성 행은 서버만 쓴다.) `generated_videos`, `canvas_generated_images`, and `highlight_jobs` are the rows that refunds (`credit_reference_id`) and server-side fetches (`video_url`) trust, so only the server writes them.

1. **Every write goes through `createAdminClient()` with an explicit owner.** Inserts carry `user_id: user.id`; updates and deletes carry `.eq("user_id", user.id)`. The admin client skips RLS, so that condition is the only isolation, and a route that reads the row first reads it with the same `user_id` condition rather than relying on RLS (`projects/[projectId]/generate/status` and `/finalize` used to read the latest row by `project_id` alone). Reads stay on the session client. Paths with no requesting user (the `cron/*` sweeps, the cut callbacks, account deletion) also use the admin client and are exempt from the `user_id` rule. The highlight side is described in `docs/flows/highlight.md`.
2. **A test pins it.** `app/lib/security/generationRowWrites.test.ts` finds every `.from("<table>")` write on the three tables and fails when one uses anything but the admin client, when a write on a request path lacks the `user_id` condition, when a builder is stored in a variable and written later, or when a table name is passed as a variable outside the known read-only admin reports. It pins the write count per file (63 today), so a new write fails until someone reads it and adds it to the list. It strips every comment, trailing ones included, before it reads a chain (a `// .eq("user_id", user.id)` tail used to count as the condition), requires the owner value to be `user.id` bound by `getClaimsUser` in the same function or a `userId` parameter that every caller fills with that `user.id`, and requires each non-null `project_id` an insert writes to be ownership-checked earlier in the same function (a `projects` query with `.eq("user_id", user.id)`, `assertHighlightProjectOwned`, or a column of a row read with that condition).
3. **Provider result URLs are checked before the server fetches them.** `/api/video/finalize`, `projects/[projectId]/generate/finalize`, `cron/video-finalize`, and the unfinalized branch of `/api/youtube/upload` pass the row's `video_url` through `assertProviderResultUrl` (`app/lib/providers/resultUrl.ts`) before `downloadVideo` or `transcodeViaKlipseCut`. Private, loopback, link-local, IPv6-literal, and metadata hosts are always refused (`assertPublicHttpUrl`). Plain `http` is refused for every known provider. Veo and Gemini Omni send an API key or OAuth token with the download, so for them any host other than exactly `generativelanguage.googleapis.com` is refused too (a `googleapis.com` suffix also let `storage.googleapis.com/<any bucket>` through). The gcp (Vertex) backend returns bytes, and a `gcsUri` only when the request sets `storageUri`, which this code does not; if we ever write to our own GCS bucket, add that bucket's path prefix only. Their `downloadVideo` runs the same check again before it reads the key or token, and because the fetch is `fetchPublic`, a redirect off that host is followed without the `x-goog-api-key` or `Authorization` header (the Veo `key=` query parameter is not carried into a `Location`). This is deliberately not `redirect: "error"`: whether the Gemini file download answers with a redirect has not been measured, refusing every redirect could break Veo and Gemini Omni downloads, and dropping the credentials on a cross-origin hop closes the leak either way. The other providers' result CDNs are not yet confirmed by measurement (Kling uses a separate CDN; MiniMax and HappyHorse mark their download as unverified in code), so a host outside their suffix list is allowed and logged with `console.warn` (`[provider-result-url]`); the list is to be fixed from those logs. That check reads only the URL string, so the download itself goes through `fetchPublic` (`app/lib/security/safeFetch.ts`) in all seven providers' `downloadVideo`: it resolves the host and refuses it if any address is private (`dns.promises.lookup` with `all`), fetches with `redirect: "manual"`, and follows at most three redirects itself, repeating the URL, DNS, and https checks on every hop and dropping request headers once a hop leaves the first origin. The private ranges include `100.64.0.0/10` and `198.18.0.0/15`, and on the IPv6 side the IPv4-translated `::ffff:0:0/96` (judged by the IPv4 inside, like `::ffff:a.b.c.d`) and the local-use NAT64 `64:ff9b:1::/48` (refused whole). What it cannot stop is DNS rebinding between its lookup and the connection fetch makes; pinning the address needs an undici dispatcher, which is a new dependency. The user routes answer a refused URL with `502 Provider video URL rejected` and leave the row as it is; the cron closes the row as `failed` without a refund, so it does not keep taking one of its five slots, and reports it with `Sentry.captureMessage` (warning, with `videoId`, `userId`, `provider`, `creditReferenceId`) so a real provider result that the check refused can be refunded by hand. `app/lib/providers/resultUrl.test.ts` pins every fetch site and that a check on the same value passes before it in the same branch (a bare statement or a `try` whose `catch` returns or continues, both only for a throwing check whose name starts with `assert`, or an `if (!check(x))` that exits; not merely an earlier line, and not a boolean check whose result is dropped), that the seven `downloadVideo` use `fetchPublic` with `httpsOnly` and no raw `fetch`, and that every raw server `fetch` whose first argument is not a string or template literal is on a list, whatever the argument is named. On that list, the two our-storage sites (the YouTube upload and Gemini Omni's input video) must sit behind their storage check and use `redirect: "error"`; the rest are recorded as they are, which does not make them safe (the image providers' reference-image fetches in `nano-banana` and `openai` are among them). Two other server fetches of a request-body URL also go through `fetchPublic` with `httpsOnly`: the custom thumbnail in `/api/youtube/upload` (`thumbnailUrl`, which used to get only the string check and a plain `fetch` that followed redirects) and the image in `POST /api/prompt/assist` (which had its own hostname regex, missed `100.64.0.0/10`, and followed redirects). The canvas sends the thumbnail image node's URL, our public CDN over https, so it still passes, and a refused thumbnail still only skips the thumbnail. Prompt assist still reads a `data:` URL inline; a plain `http` image URL is now refused.
4. **Deploy order.** This code works whether the write policies are still open or already revoked: the admin client is the service role, which RLS and the table grants do not restrict, and the explicit `user_id` conditions are no wider than what the owner policies allowed (those also let a project owner write another user's row in their project; the code does not). The revoke is a separate hand-applied migration that drops the `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, and `DELETE` policies on the three tables, revokes every table privilege from `anon`, `authenticated`, and `PUBLIC`, and grants `SELECT` back to `authenticated` only. Before it, run its read-only audit on both projects: the revoke stops new writes but leaves rows already planted, and the refund paths still trust them. The audit looks for future or pre-account timestamps, rows under another user's project, one `credit_reference_id` on several rows, references with no matching `capture` in the owner's `user_token_transactions`, refunds of references whose row completed, and the hosts `video_url` points at; closing a confirmed row as `failed` without a refund is a separate statement run only after the owner confirms. Also check for `SECURITY DEFINER` functions and non-`security_invoker` views that touch the three tables and are open to `authenticated`, because the revoke does not reach them. Apply it only after this code is live: deploy the code to prd and dev, confirm the ECS rollout is `COMPLETED`, apply the migration to **dev** and run one video generation, one image generation, one highlight analysis, one edit-node result save, and one project deletion there, then apply it to **prd**. Applied the other way round, every session-client write that still existed would fail at once. No browser code writes these tables directly (the only browser client is `app/lib/supabase/client.ts`), so an old tab only ever reaches the server routes.

## Important coupling points

- `CreateScene`, `useCreateSceneController`, and `node-canvas-v2` still share the same page lifecycle even though canvas persistence moved to `/api/canvas`.
- `useCanvasPersistence.ts`, `/api/canvas/[projectId]`, and the shape of the canvas document must stay aligned.
- Image generation, image upload persistence, and node deletion cleanup share `canvas_generated_images` and storage-key conventions.
- Sound generation uses the same recovery regime on `canvas_generated_audio`: a `bgm-{idempotencyKey}` credit reference, a `generating` row carrying `node_id`, a `GET` lookup, and canvas-load backfill. Anything that changes the credit-reference prefix breaks recovery on both ends silently — `app/lib/bgm/generationRecord.ts` owns that pair and its test locks it.
- **`node-canvas-v2/index.tsx`** rasterizes text overlays to PNG and uploads before compose (`wrap_max_width` forwarded when set), aligning preview typography with raster output closer than the drawtext-only path alone.
- **`VideoEditModal`** preview uses the normalized width/height box for layout; inner sync effects should not inflate modal undo stacks. Image overlays use the saved box sizing, not only the intrinsic source aspect.
- Overlay placement is preview-first/freeform (inspector X/Y sliders removed); drag stays partially off-frame when needed with a small visible sliver. Arrow-key nudge works when focus is not in another control.
- **One output point per node, and it opens a menu.** Every node's right side shows a single output point; clicking it opens the "next step" menu (`components/NodeNextStepMenu.tsx`), where each item says what it will create before it is chosen. The video node's `edit-out` and the composition node's `video-out` are the only visible video outputs. `youtube-out` and `instagram-out` still exist, stacked invisibly on the same spot, because saved edges, `publishHelpers`, and `hasYoutubeOutEdge` key off those handle ids. A drag from the visible output onto a publish node's input is rewritten to the matching seam by `lib/singleOutputHandle.ts`, in both `isValidConnection` and `onConnect`. The node-chrome table (`lib/nodeChrome.ts`) is the single record of this: `NODE_HANDLE_STACKS` lists only the visible output, `NODE_OUTPUT_SEAMS` lists the seams stacked on it, and `singleOutputHandle` reads its hosts from there. The archive thumbnail reads the same table, so it draws the single point and routes an edge saved on a seam from the visible output; `nodeChrome.test.ts` fails if the order in which a node file renders its `<Handle>`s in the product drifts from the table (a handle held in a variable is counted where that variable is rendered, not where it is declared). A reverse drag (from an input) dropped on the output picks the element under the pointer, and the seams take no pointer events, so it lands on the visible output. Dropped near the output, React Flow picks the closest handle, the three tie on distance, and the first in DOM order wins; the seams are not connectable, so a seam that wins rejects the drop. That is why the visible output comes first in the product DOM. The landing node mockup shows the same single output. After a menu item creates and connects a node, `revealNewNode` fits the source and the new node (zooming in no further than 100% or the current zoom) and selects the new node; if a publish node made from this video already exists, it is revealed instead of duplicated. A pending reveal is dropped when another one starts, when the canvas unmounts, or when the new node is gone by the time it runs. Marketing embeds do not move the camera. "Add to an edit", and the composition node's own input buttons (the audio menu and the image button), go through the same plan check as dragging into a composition node (`lib/editInputPlanFeature.ts`, called by `onConnect`, `makeAddEditNode`, `makeAddAudioNode`, and `onAddImageOverlayNode` with the type of the node being connected; a sound effect is a `bgm` node): sound and TTS need `editor_tts`, an image needs `editor_image_overlay`, and a blocked user gets the upgrade dialog and no node. Every output point gets its name, tooltip, and keyboard behavior from `useNextStepTrigger`. It is a disclosure button (`aria-expanded`, and `aria-controls` while the menu is open), not a menu button, because the items are reached with Tab rather than arrow keys. Tab reaches it; Enter or Space opens the menu and goes no further (no node selection, no Space pan); Tab moves through the items, and focus leaving the point and the menu closes it. When the menu closes with focus inside it (an item chosen, Escape), focus returns to the output point without scrolling the canvas, and a mouse user gets no focus ring or tooltip from it. The menu opens to the left of the point when there is no room on the right, by the same rule the input-button menus use (`getViewportAdaptiveMenuSide`). The input-button menus on a node's left side draw their items with the same part (`components/NodeMenuItem.tsx`: icon, name, and a result line), and the result line depends on the button pressed (`lib/inputMenuHints.ts`, copy `sideButtonsV2.inputMenu`): 「이미지 업로드」 and 「AI 이미지 생성」 say, in plain words, that the video starts with the picture (Start frame), ends with it (End frame), takes in the people and mood in it (Reference images), or that a new image is made from it (an image node's image button), naming the picture as 내 사진 for upload and AI로 만든 이미지 for generate (「AI 이미지 생성」 carries the sparkle, like 「AI 프롬프트 생성」 and the toolbar's image menu; until 2026-10-08 it was an up arrow next to the upload arrow, and the two could not be told apart); 「텍스트」 says the prompt is typed by hand and 「AI 프롬프트 생성」 that a short draft is refined by AI (the node sends the draft to `/api/prompt/assist`, which expands it into a full prompt). The first wording (2026-10-08 morning) read as too technical to the owner and was replaced the same day. Until 2026-10-08 those items had a name only, so the menu did not say what the picked image would do. Upload creates an image node holding the file and generate creates an empty image node, both connected to the pressed handle (`makeAddImageNode`). The video-input upload menu keeps its spec line instead. With the result line those menus are 251–351px wide, so their left/right decision uses 360 (`MENU_WIDTH_PX` in `NodeSideButtons.tsx`; it was 160). On the interactive canvas the left/right decision measures the space left by the canvas chrome, not just the viewport: an expanded layer panel takes `LAYERS_PANEL_OPEN_INLINE_END_PX` on the left and a docked help panel takes `HELP_PANEL_OPEN_INLINE_END_PX` on the right (`nodeMenuChromeInsets` in `lib/nodeMenuChrome.ts`, fed by `NodeMenuChromeContext`), so a menu next to an open panel opens to the free side; the output-point menu uses the same rule. **Node menus draw in the browser top layer.** Every `MetaDropdownPanel` inside a node on the interactive canvas (input-button menus, the next-step menu, the node toolbar dropdowns) puts its surface in a `popover="manual"` shell. A node lives inside the React Flow wrapper, which sets an inline `position: relative; z-index: 0`, so no z-index inside a node could rise above the wrapper's siblings — the layer panel (1000) and its progressive blur, the help panel (1002), the toolbar (1003) — and until 2026-10-08 those covered the menus. The shell stays a DOM child of the anchor, so outside-click checks (`contains`), Tab order into the menu, React events, wheel handling and inherited theme variables are unchanged; only the paint layer and coordinate system move. An invisible placeholder inside the anchor takes the surface's size so the anchor keeps its CSS placement (side, vertical flip, zoom compensation), and while the menu is mounted the shell is moved to the anchor's screen box every animation frame, so it follows pan, zoom and node moves. Landing, embeds, static sections and `staticOpen` previews get no context and still draw the menu inside the node.
- **Canvas help is a side panel, not a modal.** The toolbar's help button (a ? like the node buttons, labelled 도움말) and 사용법 보기 on the empty canvas open `components/CanvasHelpPanel.tsx`: a 340px panel docked on the right below 48px and above the zoom control, or a bottom sheet with a dimmed backdrop below 768px that traps focus and closes on Esc. The sheet is a fixed 72dvh tall; it used to be content height, so its top edge jumped on every page change. When the docked panel opens and nodes on screen reach under it, the canvas steps aside (`helpPanelAvoid`, `lib/canvasHelpPanelGeometry.ts`): it pans left by the overlap if that keeps them clear of an open layer panel, otherwise it fits them between the two panels the way 위치 보기 does (zooming out only). Closing the panel returns to the view it had when it opened, even if it closes while still stepping aside, unless the user moved the canvas in between (a drag, a wheel, or trackpad momentum, read from `onMoveStart` and `onMove` events that carry a source event) or 위치 보기 moved it (`interrupted`). Before 2026-10-08 only the empty-canvas cards stepped aside, so the image node's right button that step 2 names sat under the panel. It never covers the whole canvas, so a user can read a step and do it. Open/closed lives in `CreateScene` (`isNodeGuideOpen`); the current page lives in the canvas, and reopening resumes the page that was last shown. Changing page moves focus to the new page title. Every node header that has a help page shows the same ? button (`NodeHeaderActions`, through `CanvasHelpContext`), which opens the panel on that node's page; landing and embeds get no context and therefore no button. The first page has three groups and only the first has a heading; the others are separated by a hairline (no small grey eyebrow headings): 「첫 영상 만들기」, three steps with short names (이미지 만들기, 이미지를 영상으로 잇기, 영상 생성하기) whose one-sentence instruction is shown only under the current step (make an image; from the image node's right button choose 「영상으로 만들기」, or — when a video node already exists, e.g. the user started from the empty canvas's 비디오 생성 card — connect the image to that node's start frame button instead of making another; write the video and press the generate button) whose state is computed from the canvas each render (`lib/firstVideoProgress.ts`, nothing is stored; a generated or generating video counts the earlier steps as done), with 위치 보기 on the current step and the status announced to screen readers; once all three are done the group is hidden. The steps carry no badge: a muted list number, the name in medium weight and primary ink for the current step, muted for a finished one and secondary for one still ahead, and a finished step ends its line with a 12px check and 「완료」 in muted caption text (owner's pick, 2026-10-08). Task pages use the same list (one definition, `STEP_LIST` and `STEP_ROW`): a muted number, the sentence, and at the line's end the plan that step needs (「Lite 이상」, the same words as the first page's task trail) when an optional step is blocked (`helpTaskStepPlans`; `HELP_TASK_PLAN.optional` ties each requirement to its step, and a test checks the step exists in all three locales). It is left off when the whole task is already blocked by the same plan. Before 2026-10-08 task pages used grey number discs, invisible against the panel in dark, and a partly blocked task said only 「일부 단계는 …」 without saying which step; the YouTube task named no scheduling step at all, so it gained a fourth step, turning on 「예약 업로드」 before uploading. Blue stays on the primary button only, as on the onboarding survey's step bar. (Earlier the markers were an empty ring, a dotted ring and a check, which read as radio buttons, and then blue numbered circles, where a finished step and the current one were the same blue disc competing for attention.) Then the task rows, then node types, shortcuts, model comparison and FAQ, plus a search box. 위치 보기 moves the camera to the step's node and selects it (for step 2, the image node whose right-button menu is meant, or the existing video node in the connect case), fitting it into the area left of the panel on wide screens and closing the sheet first on narrow ones; when the node does not exist yet it rings the toolbar button that creates it for 2.4s. Plan lines come from `lib/canvasHelp.ts` (`HELP_TASK_PLAN`, `blockedRequirementPlan`): a task whose core needs a feature the plan lacks says 「{플랜} 이상」, one where only optional steps are blocked says so on its page, nothing is said before `usePlanGating().isReady` or for a plan already held. Sound tasks are core-gated on `editor_tts`, because generating, uploading and adding sound to a composition all read that flag. The model comparison is read from `VIDEO_MODELS` (pickable models only), never typed by hand. It is a table of only the values that differ between models — longest length, highest resolution (`helpMaxResolution`, compared by value, not list order), reference image count — with column names that stay pinned while the table scrolls; plan, a required start frame and video input sit under the model name. Values every model shares (sound, end frame) are left out of the table and said once above it, naming the exceptions (`helpModelExceptions`), because twelve repeats of the same value buried the differences. The FAQ is the LOUIS FAQ Row (md): questions only (every title is a question; four were statements until 2026-10-08), an answer opens on press, and a search hit on a question opens the page with that question open (`{ kind: "faq", item }`). Node pages keep each description to one line and drop it where the button name already says it. The shortcuts page draws keys in boxes and hand actions (drag, click, scroll, pinch) as text, joins keys with +, and hides Backspace on a Mac. Copy is `create.nodeCanvas.help`, in the official tone (no colloquial verbs), names things exactly as the screen does, calls the credit button 생성 버튼 throughout, and breaks multi-sentence descriptions one sentence per line. Usage is logged as `canvas_help_used` (`action` open/page/show_me), a diagnostic event like the onboarding hints. While the panel is open the onboarding hints are held. Node pages put, in front of each input row, the same tile as the canvas's left button for it (channel colour `--node-handle-icon-*` chosen by `getEdgeTone`, glyph from `HANDLE_GLYPH`), so the name in the help can be matched to the button on the canvas; `HELP_NODE_INPUT_HANDLES` in `lib/canvasHelp.ts` maps rows to handle ids; tests check the row count against all three locales and the handles, in order, against the canvas's `NODE_HANDLE_STACKS`. A text node connects to exactly the nodes that draw a text button on the left: `isValidConnection` reads `acceptsTextInput` (`lib/nodeChrome.ts`, derived from `NODE_HANDLE_STACKS`) instead of keeping its own list. The hand-written list had left out the sound node, so its text button was drawn but a text node could not be connected to it (fixed 2026-10-07); the sound node uses the connected text as its prompt (`connectedText`). The help calls the controls what the screen shows — the left buttons and the right button — not input or output points. Answers and task pages that point elsewhere end in a text link to that page (「모델 비교 보기」), the back button returns to the page you came from (a node page opened from 「노드 종류」 goes back to that list; it is cleared when a node header opens a page), and a task that needs a higher plan offers 「요금제 보기」, which closes the panel and goes to the subscription page (where the canvas upgrade dialog's 구독하러 가기 also goes). The current step is marked by its icon and weight, not by a surface, so its button does not sit on a second surface. On close, focus returns to the button that opened the panel even in browsers that do not focus a clicked button (Safari, Firefox), because the canvas passes the opener along. The onboarding hints stay held while the panel is open on purpose: the panel's 「첫 영상 만들기」 already shows the same path, and two guides at once would compete.
- Source/track and result-track trims move continuously during drag (no magnetic snap on the result track mid-drag); release snaps to frames. Linked clips keep matching source-region trim bounds during result moves.
- Source/track trim interaction and persisted compose trim metadata must stay consistent with `VideoOverlay` timelines and edit sessions.
- **One model catalog, one ID namespace.** `VIDEO_MODEL_CATALOG` (`app/lib/generation-v2/model-catalog.ts`) is the only place a model's supported resolutions, ratios, durations, and sound mode are written down; the canvas table (`node-canvas-v2/types.ts`) spreads them through `capabilitiesOf()` and keeps only presentation fields (label, badges, `videoInputRoles`, reference-image caps). Canvas model IDs *are* profile IDs, so `modelId` in a canvas document, `modelProfileId` in a generate request, and `generated_videos.model_profile_id` all carry the same value. Older documents still hold the pre-unification spellings (`kling-v3`, `kling-v2.6`, `veo-3.1-pro`, and the earlier `-std`/`-pro` variants); two paired legacy tables fold them — `LEGACY_VIDEO_MODEL_ID_MAP` on the canvas side (display) and `LEGACY_MODEL_ID_ALIASES` in `resolve.ts` (generation). Drop entries from both or neither: with only one, an old node opens on screen but generation fails with `Unmapped canvas model ID`.
- `/api/video/generate`, `/api/video/status`, and `/api/video/finalize` are one pipeline; changing status semantics in one route requires updating the others.
- Project creation plus image/video/highlight generation routes emit best-effort first-party product events for admin funnel, activation, and retention metrics. See `docs/flows/admin-product-metrics.md` for event definitions.
- Seedance status parsing is intentionally tolerant because ModelArk task responses can expose task id/status/video URL at top-level or under `data`/`content`; keep provider-specific parsing inside `app/lib/providers/seedance`.
- Seedance caps are per model version: 2.0 Fast/Mini support 480p/720p, 2.0 Standard adds 1080p/4K, and 2.5 tops out at 1080p (no 4K). Image inputs — including first/last frames and reference images — are capped at 9 for the 2.0 series and 30 for 2.5; reference videos at 3 (15s combined) for 2.0 and 10 (30s combined) for 2.5. The provider (`SEEDANCE_MODEL_LIMITS`) enforces these, and the canvas capability table now **derives** the resolution / ratio / duration / sound lists from the same profiles instead of repeating them (see the model-catalog coupling point below). First-frame vs. reference inputs are mutually exclusive on every version.
- Veo image-to-video downloads reference images server-side, so accepted image URLs are intentionally restricted to this project's Supabase Storage object/render paths over HTTPS. Its initial `generateVideos` SDK request is also bounded by an app-level timeout so generation routes do not wait indefinitely for provider submission.
- `/api/projects/[projectId]/detail` is the cross-feature publish/handoff endpoint from create into publish and analytics—not a standalone “detail scene” viewer.
- Publish nodes must not persist optimistic “uploaded” UI state before a platform job has returned its durable video/media id; processing and failed job rows from `/api/projects/[projectId]/detail` are part of the canvas resume contract.
- `VideoEditModal` keeps the preview editing floor on short viewports by compacting the timeline/footer and scrolling the left workspace before shrinking preview first.
